                    briefing_text_r1:       `The Aurora seat passed the mandatory regulatory test but <strong>failed Northwind's own margin-of-safety standard</strong> in 3 of 40 high-temperature sled tests. It is legal to sell — and below the bar your brand has always promised. A late cost-down foam substitution saved CA$1.8M and narrowed the margin. Don't frame this as 'ship vs. delay' or a pure margin call. Frame it as a <strong>character problem</strong>: which dimensions pull toward shipping (Drive, the deadline) and which toward delaying (<em>Courage, Temperance, Accountability</em>), which is most likely <em>over-played</em> under board pressure, and where Judgment must do the integrating work.`,
                    briefing_title_r2:      `Round 2 — Weigh the dimensions and reach a decision`,
                    briefing_text_r2:       `Make the call: ship on time, delay-and-fix (CA$4.2M, 7-week slip), or a structured alternative. But show <strong>how character drove it</strong>, with at least four dimensions <em>in balance</em> — no single one dominating. Drive without Temperance is recklessness; Courage without Humility is grandstanding. Name the trade-off explicitly: what is sacrificed and which dimension justifies it. Missing the holiday window costs an estimated CA$22M; shipping the unfixed seat risks a CA$30–80M recall and the brand's core promise.`,
                    briefing_title_r3:      `Round 3 — Stakeholder confrontation and holding the line`,
                    briefing_text_r3:       `Your decision meets resistance. The <strong>board chair</strong> demands you protect margin and the date; the <strong>Head of Quality</strong> who raised the flag is watching whether you back the data and the person; a <strong>big-box buyer</strong> threatens to pull the end-cap. Demonstrate character in live pressure — <em>Courage</em> to deliver an unwelcome message, <em>Justice and Humanity</em> toward the whistle-raiser, <em>Integrity</em> with the retailer, <em>Humility</em> to revise if new data warrants. Hold the line — or adapt it for the right reasons, not to please the loudest voice.`,
                    briefing_title_r4:      `Round 4 — Outcome, decision quality and building character capacity`,
                    briefing_text_r4:       `The consequences land: the holiday window closed and the quarter missed target. Now separate <strong>decision quality from outcome</strong> — a sound, well-balanced decision can still have a bad short-term result, and judging leadership by outcome alone teaches you to ship the unsafe seat next time it 'works out.' A journalist asks about the foam change: <em>Integrity is not situational</em>. Diagnose which dimension, strengthened, most improves future decisions, and commit to one practice that embeds leader-character judgment in the division's routine.`,

                    r1_frame_label:         `Frame the real dilemma in one sentence. Which is it?`,
                    frame_character:        `A character problem — legality is a floor; our brand promise is the standard, and Judgment must integrate competing dimensions`,
                    frame_financial:        `A financial problem — weigh CA$22M lost sales against CA$4.2M remediation and pick the cheaper number`,
                    frame_legal:            `A legal problem — the seat passed the regulatory test, so we are compliant and we ship`,
                    frame_political:        `A political problem — manage the board's perception of me in week three above all`,
                    r1_overplay_label:      `Which dimension is most likely to be OVER-played under board margin pressure?`,
                    over_drive:             `Drive — the pull of the deadline and the margin metric does our thinking for us`,
                    over_humility:          `Humility — we will defer to the board and to whoever shouts loudest`,
                    over_temperance:        `Temperance — we will be so restrained we never decide`,
                    over_collab:            `Collaboration — we will consult everyone until the window closes`,
                    r1_underplay_label:     `Which dimension is most at risk of being UNDER-played?`,
                    under_courage:          `Courage & Accountability — to deliver an unwelcome truth and own the foam decision`,
                    under_drive:            `Drive — we will not push hard enough to hit the date`,
                    under_justice2:         `Justice — but only toward the board, not the whistle-raiser`,
                    under_none:             `None — under deadline pressure character takes care of itself`,
                    r1_judgment_label:      `How explicitly will your team make Judgment do the integrating work, naming dimensions rather than reacting? (%)`,
                    r1_judgment_readout:    `Deliberate, articulable character reasoning:`,

                    r2_action_label:        `Make the call. What is the action?`,
                    act_fix:                `Delay-and-fix — change the foam, absorb CA$4.2M and the 7-week slip, protect the safety standard`,
                    act_phased:             `Structured alternative — transparent phased replacement: ship a verified-safe sub-batch, renegotiate end-caps, fix the rest`,
                    act_ship:               `Ship on time — it passed the regulatory test; protect the margin and the launch date`,
                    act_conceal:            `Ship quietly — do not document the failed internal tests; assume they stay internal`,
                    r2_balance_label:       `Weight the four character dimensions that drive the decision — must total 100 (no single one above 45)`,
                    r2_weight_readout:      `Weighting allocated:`,
                    r2_weight_cap:          `/ 100`,
                    dim_courage:            `Courage — deliver the unwelcome truth to the board`,
                    dim_temperance:         `Temperance — resist the deadline and margin metric`,
                    dim_accountability:     `Accountability — own the foam-substitution decision`,
                    dim_collaboration:      `Collaboration — bring Engineering's data and explore options`,
                    r2_tradeoff_label:      `Name the trade-off explicitly: what is sacrificed, and which dimension justifies it?`,
                    trade_margin:           `Sacrifice this quarter's margin and the holiday window; Courage and Integrity justify protecting the safety promise`,
                    trade_none:             `Sacrifice nothing — claim we can hit the date and the standard with no real trade-off`,
                    trade_safety:           `Sacrifice the internal safety margin; Drive justifies hitting the committed date`,

                    r3_board_label:         `The board chair demands you protect margin and the date. You…`,
                    board_defend:           `Defend the decision with its character logic and the data — courage with respect, not capitulation or war`,
                    board_cave:             `Cave — reverse to shipping on time to keep the chair happy in week three`,
                    board_war:              `Go to war single-handed — kill the launch, no data, no compromise, make an enemy of the chair`,
                    r3_quality_label:       `The Head of Quality who raised the flag is watching. You…`,
                    qual_back:              `Back the data and the person publicly — thank them for the courage to raise it, protect psychological safety`,
                    qual_neutral:           `Stay neutral — acknowledge the flag privately but make no visible statement`,
                    qual_sideline:          `Overrule and sideline them — make clear concerns are unwelcome before a launch`,
                    r3_retailer_label:      `The big-box buyer threatens to pull the end-cap. You…`,
                    ret_honest:             `Tell the truth, offer a credible new date and a phased plan — protect the relationship and the standard`,
                    ret_bluff:              `Promise the original date anyway to keep the slot, and hope the fix lands in time`,
                    ret_cave:               `Cave to the buyer — ship to hold the end-cap regardless of the safety standard`,
                    r3_humility_label:      `Humility check: new data shows the fix can be done in 5 weeks, not 7. How much do you let evidence revise your plan? (%)`,
                    r3_humility_readout:    `Willingness to revise on evidence, not ego:`,

                    r4_judge_label:         `The quarter missed target. How do you judge the decision?`,
                    judge_separate:         `Separate quality from outcome — sound, well-balanced reasoning stands even when timing hurt the result`,
                    judge_outcome:          `Outcome-only — we missed the number, so we failed and should have shipped`,
                    judge_luck:             `Take full credit — we always knew it would work out, no luck involved`,
                    r4_disclose_label:      `A journalist asks about the foam change. Your stance?`,
                    disc_transparent:       `Transparent and accountable — explain the standard, the test data and the fix; Integrity is not situational`,
                    disc_minimal:           `Minimal — confirm only what is asked, volunteer nothing`,
                    disc_deny:              `Deny relevance — the seat was legal, there is nothing to discuss`,
                    r4_capacity_label:      `Which dimension, if strengthened, most improves the division's future decisions?`,
                    cap_courage:            `Courage, Temperance & Accountability under pressure — embed them in routine decisions`,
                    cap_drive:              `Drive — push even harder on deadlines next time`,
                    cap_none:               `None — this was a one-off, no capacity-building needed`,
                    r4_practice_label:      `One practice to embed leader-character judgment: how firmly do you commit to it (e.g. a character check in every major decision review)? (%)`,

                    warn_judgment_range:    `Your Judgment-integration level must be a percentage between 0 and 100.`,
                    warn_weight_negative:   `Character-dimension weights cannot be negative.`,
                    warn_weight_total:      `The four dimension weights must total exactly 100. Re-balance.`,
                    warn_weight_dominate:   `No single dimension may exceed 45 — that is the vice of one dimension dominating. Re-balance.`,
                    warn_humility_range:    `Your revise-on-evidence level must be a percentage between 0 and 100.`,
                    warn_practice_range:    `Your commitment level must be a percentage between 0 and 100.`,

                    coach_default:          `Decision logged.`,
                    coach_overbudget:       `<strong>You blew through the CA$6.0M envelope.</strong> A scramble for a crude saving to stay in budget re-opened the very risk you were closing. Temperance applies to spending too — a sound fix doesn't need to overrun.`,

                    coach_r1_frame_character: `<strong>That's the right frame.</strong> Legality is a floor, not the standard — your entire brand is 'beyond the minimum.' Treating this as a character problem, with Judgment integrating the dimensions, is what lets you reach a decision you can actually defend, teach and repeat.`,
                    coach_r1_frame_financial: `<strong>A pure CA$22M-vs-CA$4.2M calculation misses the point.</strong> The cheaper number isn't the right number for a child-safety brand. This is a character problem first; the money is one input to Judgment, not the whole decision.`,
                    coach_r1_frame_legal:     `<strong>Legality is a floor, not your standard.</strong> The seat passed the regulatory test and still fell below the safety bar you promise. 'We're compliant, so we ship' is exactly the reasoning a cold-February field incident makes indefensible.`,
                    coach_r1_frame_political: `<strong>Managing your week-three image is not leadership.</strong> Make the perception the goal and you'll under-weight Courage and Accountability precisely when they matter most. Frame the decision, not your reputation.`,
                    coach_r1_overplay_wrong:  `<strong>Look again at what the pressure amplifies.</strong> Under a cost-cutting board, the dimension that quietly does your thinking for you is <em>Drive</em> — the pull of the deadline and the margin metric. Name it, or it runs the decision unchecked by Temperance.`,
                    coach_r1_diag_sharp:      `<strong>Sharp diagnosis.</strong> You framed it as a character problem, named Drive as the over-played dimension under board pressure, and Courage and Accountability as most at risk of being under-played. That is exactly the map Judgment needs to integrate the call.`,
                    coach_r1_lowjudgment:     `<strong>This is a hunch, not character.</strong> If you can't make Judgment name the dimensions explicitly, you'll reach a decision you can feel but can't defend to the board — or teach, or repeat. Make the reasoning articulable.`,

                    coach_r2_fix:           `<strong>Delay-and-fix protects the standard.</strong> You absorb CA$4.2M and a 7-week slip rather than carry a known sub-standard seat into a cold-weather field. Now make sure the character logic — Courage, Temperance, Accountability — is balanced, not a single heroic dimension.`,
                    coach_r2_phased:        `<strong>A structured alternative can be sound</strong> — a transparent phased replacement protects the standard while salvaging part of the window. It carries more execution risk than a clean fix, so the character balance and the trade-off have to be airtight.`,
                    coach_r2_ship:          `<strong>You hit the date and the margin — and carried the risk into the field.</strong> Legality is a floor, not the standard. Which dimension did you under-weight when Drive and the deadline were doing your thinking? A cold-February collision in exactly your flagged conditions makes this indefensible.`,
                    coach_r2_conceal:       `<strong>You shipped quietly and didn't document the trade-off.</strong> But the foam substitution and the 3-of-40 failures are already in writing. In discovery, a journalist's inbox or a regulator's file, this reads as concealment — Integrity isn't situational, and you've converted a risk call into an indefensible ethical breach.`,
                    coach_r2_balanced:      `<strong>Four dimensions in balance — that's Judgment working.</strong> No single dimension dominates: Courage to tell the truth, Temperance to resist the deadline, Accountability to own the foam call, Collaboration to bring the data. That balance is exactly what makes the decision both right and defensible.`,
                    coach_r2_dominate:      `<strong>One dimension is dominating.</strong> A single over-activated dimension becomes a vice — Drive becomes recklessness, Courage becomes grandstanding. Spread the weight so Judgment integrates the dimensions rather than letting one run the show.`,
                    coach_r2_tradenone:     `<strong>'No trade-off' is a fantasy.</strong> Every real decision under pressure sacrifices something. Claiming you can hit the date and the standard with no cost means you haven't faced the dilemma — name what you give up and which dimension justifies it.`,
                    coach_r2_tradesafety:   `<strong>Sacrificing the safety margin is the one trade you can't make.</strong> Drive does not justify shipping below your own standard for a child-safety product. That's Drive unchecked by Temperance and Courage — the textbook vice of an over-played dimension.`,

                    coach_r3_defend:        `<strong>Courage with respect, not capitulation or war.</strong> You defended the decision with its character logic and the data to a hostile chair without grandstanding or caving. That is exactly how Courage and Humility work together under board pressure.`,
                    coach_r3_cave:          `<strong>You reversed to keep the chair happy.</strong> Caving in week three teaches the board that pressure moves you and re-opens the field risk you'd closed. Temperance and Courage exist precisely for this moment — don't trade them for a comfortable meeting.`,
                    coach_r3_war:           `<strong>You went to war single-handed.</strong> Brave — but you didn't bring Engineering's full data, didn't explore the compromise, and made an enemy of the chair on day one. Courage without Humility and Collaboration isn't character; it's grandstanding, and it costs you.`,
                    coach_r3_back:          `<strong>You backed the data and the person.</strong> Thanking the Head of Quality for the courage to raise the flag protects psychological safety — and teaches 1,400 people that surfacing a safety concern is valued, not career-limiting. Justice and Humanity compound into organisational capacity.`,
                    coach_r3_sideline:      `<strong>You sidelined the person who raised the flag.</strong> You may have saved this launch, but you just taught 1,400 people that raising a safety concern is career-limiting — in a company whose entire brand is safety. What happens the next time someone sees a problem?`,
                    coach_r3_bluff:         `<strong>Promising the original date you can't hit is a small Integrity leak.</strong> If the fix slips you've burned the retailer's trust and your own word. Manage the relationship with the truth and a credible plan, not a hopeful bluff.`,
                    coach_r3_retailcave:    `<strong>Caving to the buyer trades the safety standard for an end-cap.</strong> A retail slot is recoverable; a sub-standard child seat in the field is not. Protect the relationship without compromising the one thing your brand exists to promise.`,
                    coach_r3_norevise:      `<strong>Refusing to revise on new evidence is ego, not conviction.</strong> The 5-week data is exactly the kind of information Humility should let update your plan. Holding the line is a virtue; holding it against the evidence is a vice.`,
                    coach_r3_overrevise:    `<strong>Revising completely at the first new data point is its own instability.</strong> Humility weighs evidence; it doesn't abandon a sound decision the moment someone pushes. Integrate the new data into the plan, don't let it overturn your Judgment wholesale.`,

                    coach_r4_separate:      `<strong>Decision quality is not the same as outcome.</strong> You made the right, well-balanced call with the information you had; the missed window was partly timing and luck. Judge leadership by the reasoning and the character balance, or you'll learn to ship the unsafe seat next time it 'works out.'`,
                    coach_r4_outcome:       `<strong>Judging yourself purely on the quarter is the dangerous lesson.</strong> 'We missed the number, so we should have shipped' is exactly how a leader learns to carry the next risk. A sound decision can have a bad short-term result — separate the two or you'll repeat the wrong call.`,
                    coach_r4_luck:          `<strong>Taking full credit ignores the role of luck and timing.</strong> Just as a bad outcome doesn't condemn a sound decision, a good one doesn't vindicate a reckless one. Honest Accountability means naming what was Judgment and what was fortune.`,
                    coach_r4_transparent:   `<strong>Integrity is not situational.</strong> Explaining the standard, the test data and the fix to the journalist closes the concealment risk entirely and turns the story into evidence of a brand that means what it says. That is Accountability and Integrity holding under public pressure.`,
                    coach_r4_minimal:       `<strong>Volunteering nothing reads as something to hide.</strong> 'Confirm only what's asked' is a half-step from concealment and keeps the reputational risk alive. A safety brand defends its promise by being transparent, not minimal.`,
                    coach_r4_deny:          `<strong>Denying relevance is concealment by another name.</strong> 'It was legal, there's nothing to discuss' is precisely the line that reads as a cover-up on the front page. Integrity isn't situational — this converts a defensible decision into an indefensible one.`,
                    coach_r4_capdrive:      `<strong>More Drive is not the lesson here.</strong> Drive was the dimension already over-played under deadline pressure. The capacity that needs building is Courage, Temperance and Accountability — the dimensions hardest to sustain when the board wants margin.`,
                    coach_r4_capnone:       `<strong>'A one-off, no capacity-building needed' guarantees a repeat.</strong> The next deadline-vs-values collision is coming. Character is a set of habits that can be developed — build the capacity now so the next call is handled from strength, not improvised under pressure.`,

                    sum_default:            `Decision made.`,
                    sum_r1_good:            `Framed as a character problem; named Drive over, Courage under.`,
                    sum_r1_weak:            `Mis-framed the dilemma or mis-named the dimensions in tension.`,
                    sum_r2_good:            `Sound action with four dimensions in balance and an honest trade-off.`,
                    sum_r2_breach:          `Shipped without disclosure — an Integrity breach.`,
                    sum_r2_weak:            `Wrong action, one dimension dominating, or a dishonest trade-off.`,
                    sum_r3_good:            `Held the line: defended, backed the whistle-raiser, honest with the retailer.`,
                    sum_r3_punished:        `Sidelined the person who raised the flag — damaged psychological safety.`,
                    sum_r3_weak:            `Caved, grandstanded, or compromised the standard under pressure.`,
                    sum_r4_good:            `Separated quality from outcome; transparent; built character capacity.`,
                    sum_r4_weak:            `Outcome-only judgment, minimal disclosure, or no capacity built.`,

                    insight_breach:         `Integrity breach: you shipped a seat below your own standard without disclosing the failed tests — and the foam decision and the 3-of-40 results are already in writing. This is the one outcome no financial result can defend; concealment converts a risk judgment into an indefensible ethical failure.`,
                    insight_charled:        `Character-led judgment: you framed the dilemma through the character lens, decided with the dimensions in balance, held the line under live pressure and protected the people and the standard. This is a decision you can defend to the board, teach the division, and repeat.`,
                    insight_thintrust:      `Your decision was sound and well-balanced, but stakeholder trust stayed thin — the board, the quality team or the retailer didn't come with you. Sound Judgment lands better when Justice, Humanity and Collaboration bring the stakeholders along.`,
                    insight_reactive:       `You reacted to the pressure rather than leading through it — letting Drive, the deadline or the loudest voice do your thinking. Re-examine where Judgment had to integrate the dimensions and which one you let dominate or drop.`,
                    insight_dominate:       `At some point a single dimension dominated — Drive became recklessness or Courage became grandstanding. A strength over-activated becomes a vice; balance is the whole point of the framework.`,
                    insight_punished:       `You damaged the courage of others: sidelining the Head of Quality teaches 1,400 people that raising a safety flag is career-limiting. In a safety brand, that is the capacity you can least afford to lose.`,
                    insight_risk:           `You carried significant deadline/safety risk into the field. For a child-safety brand a legally-defensible-but-sub-standard seat is an existential exposure — the CA$30–80M recall scenario is exactly what character is meant to prevent.`,
                    insight_qualnotoutcome: `A sound decision with thin trust or a bad quarter is still a sound decision — separate decision quality from outcome, or you'll learn the wrong lesson and ship the unsafe seat next time it pays off.`,
                    insight_lasts:          `This is leadership that builds capacity: balanced character, sound Judgment, trust intact and risk contained. The next deadline-vs-values collision will be handled from strength, not improvised under pressure.`,

                    hint_breach:            `You're on an Integrity-breach path — shipping below your own standard without disclosure. The failed tests are already documented; transparency and a fix are the only defensible route.`,
                    hint_dominate:          `One character dimension is dominating your decisions. Spread the weight — let Judgment integrate Courage, Temperance, Accountability and Collaboration rather than one heroic lever.`,
                    hint_punished:          `You've damaged the courage of others by sidelining the whistle-raiser. Back the data and the person, or no one raises the next flag in a company built on safety.`,
                    hint_risk:              `Deadline/safety risk is high — you're carrying a sub-standard seat or a broken promise toward the field. Reconsider the action and the trade-off before the field decides for you.`,
                    hint_behind:            `Your decision quality is lagging. Frame the call through the character lens, name the dimensions in tension, and make the reasoning articulable — a hunch can't be defended.`,
                    hint_strong:            `Strong position — balanced character, sound decision quality and stakeholder trust. Now lock in transparency and build the capacity so the next collision is handled from strength.`,

